      "text": "Correct answer Move To... This element is a more accessible alternative to drag & drop reordering. Press Enter or Space to move this question. Question 3 2.5 / 2.5 pts Edit this Question Delete this Question 0 true_false_question 111040543 When applying integration by substitution for definite integrals, <img class=\"equation_image\" title=\"\\int_a^bf\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)g'\\left(x\\right)dx=\\int_{g\\left(a\\right)}^{g\\left(b\\right)}f\\left(u\\right)du\" src=\"/equation_images/%255Cint_a%255Ebf%255Cleft(g%255Cleft(x%255Cright)%255Cright)g'%255Cleft(x%255Cright)dx%253D%255Cint_%257Bg%255Cleft(a%255Cright)%257D%255E%257Bg%255Cleft(b%255Cright)%257Df%255Cleft(u%255Cright)du\" alt=\"LaTeX: \\int_a^bf\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)g'\\left(x\\right)dx=\\int_{g\\left(a\\right)}^{g\\left(b\\right)}f\\left(u\\right)du\" data-equation-content=\"\\int_a^bf\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)g'\\left(x\\right)dx=\\int_{g\\left(a\\right)}^{g\\left(b\\right)}f\\left(u\\right)du\">, it is required to convert back to the variable <img class=\"equation_image\" title=\"x\\:\" src=\"/equation_images/x%255C%253A\" alt=\"LaTeX: x\\:\" data-equation-content=\"x\\:\">&nbsp;for the final answer.&nbsp; When applying integration by substitution for definite integrals, ∫baf(g(x))g′(x)dx=∫g(b)g(a)f(u)du\\int_a^bf\\left(g\\left(x\\right)\\right)g'\\left(x\\right)dx=\\int_{g\\left(a\\right)}^{g\\left(b\\right)}f\\left(u\\right)du, it is required to convert back to the variable xx\\: for the final answer. 2678 True True exact_answer none 59201283 2678 0 (with margin: 0) 0 (with precision: 10) Between 0 and 0 margin of error +/- 0 7748 False False exact_answer none 59201283 7748 0 (with margin: 0) 0 (with precision: 10) Between 0 and 0 margin of error +/- 100 Nice work! Yes, the conversion is NOT required. By converting the x-bounds to u-bounds, you do no need to convert back with definite integrals. Note: With indefinite integrals, we DO need to convert back."
